{{!
	PersonalMenu.mustache -- the Blitzy user menu.

	The personal tools MediaWiki provides for whoever is reading, rendered as a
	disclosure inside the floating navigation card. Reached from skin.mustache
	through NavCard.mustache, which references this partial bare, at root
	context, with no enclosing section. No context has therefore been pushed,
	and every portlet below is addressed by its full dotted path from the root
	template data.

	A note on the comment style in this file: a Mustache comment ends at the
	first closing double brace it meets, so a comment can never quote a
	Mustache tag. Template data is named here in plain prose instead.

	CONSUMED TEMPLATE DATA
	----------------------
	Four sibling portlets, each a MediaWiki\Skin\Components\SkinComponentMenu
	payload reached from data-portlets. All four are rendered, in a fixed order
	that never varies with the request:

	  1. data-portlets.data-user-page                  -> id p-user-page
	  2. data-portlets.data-notifications              -> id p-notifications
	  3. data-portlets.data-user-interface-preferences -> id p-user-interface-preferences
	  4. data-portlets.data-user-menu                  -> id p-personal

	Per portlet, the keys used are:

	  id                  Element id, emitted verbatim. data-user-menu is the
	                      one that matters most: SkinComponentMenu renames the
	                      menu user-menu to personal before deriving the id, so
	                      this key arrives already spelled p-personal.
	                      Re-emitting it unaltered is the whole mechanism by
	                      which the preserved p-personal selector survives a
	                      complete visual redesign.
	  class               mw-portlet plus mw-portlet-<name>, gaining a trailing
	                      emptyPortlet when the menu has no items and no hook
	                      content. Appended to the Blitzy class rather than
	                      replacing it, so both the core hooks and the
	                      empty-state marker pass through untouched.
	  html-tooltip        Raw attribute string produced by Linker::tooltip. It
	                      already carries its own leading space, so it is
	                      emitted with none in front of it; an absent tooltip
	                      then leaves no stray whitespace in the start tag.
	  html-before-portal  Raw HTML contributed around the list by the
	  html-after-portal   SkinAfterPortlet hook. Empty strings unless an
	                      extension has something to add, and emitted so that
	                      such content is never silently discarded.
	  array-items         The list entries. Each carries html-item, the
	                      complete list item MediaWiki built for it, including
	                      its id, its mw-list-item class, its active class when
	                      the entry is current, and the blitzy-menu-item class
	                      that Hooks\BlitzyHooks added upstream. Emitting
	                      html-item is what guarantees every entry MediaWiki
	                      supplies reaches the page: nothing here filters,
	                      reorders or curates the list.

	Sibling keys deliberately NOT consumed:

	  html-items          Byte for byte the same entries as array-items, only
	                      pre-concatenated. Emitting both would render every
	                      entry twice, so exactly one of the two is used.
	  label               data-user-menu resolves this to the translated
	                      "Personal tools", but the other three portlets have
	                      no corresponding message and fall back to their raw
	                      menu name. Exposing user-page or notifications to a
	                      screen reader would be worse than leaving the lists
	                      unnamed, so the landmark below carries the accessible
	                      name for the whole disclosure and the individual
	                      lists carry none.
	  is-empty            Already expressed in class as emptyPortlet, which is
	                      the marker gadgets and core stylesheets look for.
	  data-personal       The pre-1.46 combined menu, deprecated, and
	                      requesting it emits a deprecation warning. It is
	                      neither requested in skin.json nor read here.

	Messages, both declared in the messages array in skin.json:

	  msg-personaltools           Accessible name of the landmark and the
	                              visible text of the button.
	  msg-blitzy-personal-toggle  Fallback accessible name on the checkbox.

	FIXED IDENTIFIERS DEFINED HERE
	------------------------------
	  blitzy-personal-checkbox    The disclosure input.
	  blitzy-personal-button      The disclosure button.

	Both are literals rather than derived values, for two reasons.
	resources/skins.blitzy.js/dropdown.js binds its listeners to them and
	tests/qunit/dropdown.test.js dispatches against them, so they have to be
	predictable; and the two captures of this surface are compared byte for
	byte across consecutive runs, so nothing here may vary between renders.
	Because they are literals, this partial must be referenced exactly once per
	page, which NavCard.mustache does.

	Two consequences for anything that drives this control programmatically,
	both confirmed in a real browser against this markup. Target the checkbox,
	not the label: the stylesheet clips the input over the button, so the input
	is the topmost hit-test target and an automated click aimed at the label is
	intercepted by it. And Space activates the control natively while Enter does
	not, because the element really is a checkbox however it announces itself;
	Enter activation, dismiss on outside click and dismiss on focus loss are all
	behaviours dropdown.js adds, using the binders MediaWiki's checkboxHack
	module exports for exactly that purpose.

	WHY A CHECKBOX AND NOT A BUTTON
	-------------------------------
	The skin has to stay usable with scripting switched off, so the disclosure
	is the checkbox hack MediaWiki documents in
	resources/src/mediawiki.page.ready/checkboxHack.js. Nothing in MediaWiki
	binds to these class names on its own; opening and closing is done entirely
	by a checked-state sibling selector in
	resources/skins.blitzy.styles/components/NavCard.less. That selector is
	only expressible if the input precedes the button and the target as
	siblings, which is the order below. dropdown.js then layers
	dismiss-on-outside-click over it and keeps the expanded state current, but
	it is an enhancement: with it absent the control still opens, still closes,
	and every link inside stays reachable by keyboard.

	Appearance is entirely class-driven; this file sets no inline style and
	names no colour, and it states no direction, leaving both to the flip-aware
	stylesheet. Nothing here branches on who is reading -- the difference
	between the anonymous and the logged-in card comes only from MediaWiki
	supplying different entries in data-user-menu.
}}
<nav class="blitzy-personal-menu" aria-label="{{msg-personaltools}}">
	{{!
		The input is the real control: it is what receives focus, what the
		keyboard operates, and what assistive technology announces. The
		stylesheet clips it out of sight rather than removing it from the
		layout, so it must not be given a hidden attribute here.

		A button role, a popup hint and an expanded state together make a
		checkbox behave and announce as a disclosure button. The expanded state
		is set on the input, not on the label: since MediaWiki 1.38 the label
		form is deprecated. Its server-rendered value is the closed state,
		which is correct both for a first paint and for a reader with no
		scripting at all, and dropdown.js takes over maintaining it from there.

		aria-labelledby points at the label, so the accessible name is the same
		words a sighted reader sees. aria-label repeats the intent as a
		fallback for the case where that reference cannot be resolved, which is
		what Vector settled on for its own dropdowns.

		Two omissions are deliberate. There is no name attribute, so the input
		is never submitted with a surrounding form. There is no tabindex, so
		the element keeps its natural place in the tab order.

		autocomplete="off" stops the browser restoring the checked state on
		reload and on history navigation. Without it the menu could come back
		open on a page that was captured closed, which is the one way this
		markup could make a capture unreproducible.
	}}
	<input {{!
		}}type="checkbox" {{!
		}}id="blitzy-personal-checkbox" {{!
		}}class="mw-checkbox-hack-checkbox blitzy-personal-menu__checkbox" {{!
		}}role="button" {{!
		}}aria-haspopup="true" {{!
		}}aria-expanded="false" {{!
		}}aria-labelledby="blitzy-personal-button" {{!
		}}aria-label="{{msg-blitzy-personal-toggle}}" {{!
		}}autocomplete="off">
	{{!
		The label is the visible affordance. It is hidden from assistive
		technology because the input above already exposes the same control and
		announcing both would be a duplicate. It still carries real text: that
		text is what aria-labelledby resolves to, and it is what a reader sees
		and clicks. The inner span is a styling hook and nothing more.
	}}
	<label {{!
		}}id="blitzy-personal-button" {{!
		}}class="mw-checkbox-hack-button blitzy-personal-menu__button" {{!
		}}for="blitzy-personal-checkbox" {{!
		}}aria-hidden="true">{{!
		}}<span class="blitzy-personal-menu__button-text">{{msg-personaltools}}</span>{{!
	}}</label>
	{{!
		The disclosure target. Its four portlets are written out one after
		another rather than looped, because they are four separate keys in the
		template data rather than one list, and because their order is part of
		the contract: the same request must always produce the same document.
		Each is wrapped in a section on its own path, so a portlet MediaWiki
		did not supply produces nothing at all rather than an empty shell.
	}}
	<div class="blitzy-personal-menu__content mw-checkbox-hack-target">
		{{#data-portlets.data-user-page}}
		<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-personal-menu__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}"{{{html-tooltip}}}>{{!
			}}{{{html-before-portal}}}{{!
			}}<ul class="blitzy-personal-menu__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{!
			}}{{{html-after-portal}}}{{!
		}}</div>
		{{/data-portlets.data-user-page}}
		{{#data-portlets.data-notifications}}
		<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-personal-menu__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}"{{{html-tooltip}}}>{{!
			}}{{{html-before-portal}}}{{!
			}}<ul class="blitzy-personal-menu__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{!
			}}{{{html-after-portal}}}{{!
		}}</div>
		{{/data-portlets.data-notifications}}
		{{#data-portlets.data-user-interface-preferences}}
		<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-personal-menu__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}"{{{html-tooltip}}}>{{!
			}}{{{html-before-portal}}}{{!
			}}<ul class="blitzy-personal-menu__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{!
			}}{{{html-after-portal}}}{{!
		}}</div>
		{{/data-portlets.data-user-interface-preferences}}
		{{#data-portlets.data-user-menu}}
		<div id="{{id}}" class="blitzy-personal-menu__portlet{{#class}} {{.}}{{/class}}"{{{html-tooltip}}}>{{!
			}}{{{html-before-portal}}}{{!
			}}<ul class="blitzy-personal-menu__list">{{#array-items}}{{{html-item}}}{{/array-items}}</ul>{{!
			}}{{{html-after-portal}}}{{!
		}}</div>
		{{/data-portlets.data-user-menu}}
	</div>
</nav>
